 \subsection{Exploratory prediction for diaspore and \texorpdfstring{$\delta$}{delta}-AlOOH}
 \label{sec:alooh}
 
-Diaspore ($\alpha$-AlOOH, $Pnma$, mp-625152) transforms at high pressure to $\delta$-AlOOH ($P2_1nm$, mp-23807), which has the stronger hydrogen bond: its O-H is longer by 24 to 32~m\AA{} and its O$\cdots$O shorter by 61 to 92~m\AA{} at 0~GPa in the models. The rule therefore predicts that deuteration destabilises $\delta$-AlOOH. At 0~GPa geometry the harmonic isotope term $\ddF(\delta - \mathrm{diaspore})$ at 300~K is $+0.52$, $+0.77$ and $+0.83$~meV/atom (MatterSim, MACE, ORB), with $\Delta V = -0.30$ to $-0.32$~\AA$^3$/atom, which gives $+0.3$ to $+0.4$~GPa. Quasi-harmonic Gibbs energies at 10, 15 and 20~GPa, with one linear fit for both isotopes, place the 300~K transition of AlOOD 1.33~GPa (MACE) and 1.13~GPa (ORB) above that of AlOOH; a temperature-resolved rebuild gives $+1.33$, $+1.13$ and $+0.99$~GPa (MatterSim) at 300~K and $+0.94$, $+0.81$ and $+0.73$~GPa at 1000~K. These quasi-harmonic runs use a $\Gamma$-centred $q$ mesh with the point density of the screen, rounded to an integer (not necessarily even) between 4 and 12, rather than the shifted mesh of the screen. The quasi-harmonic isotope term at 10, 15 and 20~GPa, 2.30, 2.42 and 2.22~meV/atom (MACE) and 1.86, 2.30 and 2.50 (ORB), is about three times its 0~GPa harmonic value, which is why the pressure shift exceeds the 0~GPa estimate. Its growth with pressure is consistent with the $\delta$-phase hydrogen bond strengthening towards symmetrization, exactly where harmonic physics is least reliable; O-H and O$\cdots$O along the pressure grid were not stored and are not reported.
+Diaspore ($\alpha$-AlOOH, $Pnma$, mp-625152) transforms at high pressure to $\delta$-AlOOH ($P2_1nm$, mp-23807), which has the stronger hydrogen bond: its O-H is longer by 24 to 32~m\AA{} and its O$\cdots$O shorter by 61 to 92~m\AA{} at 0~GPa in the models. The rule therefore predicts that deuteration destabilises $\delta$-AlOOH. At 0~GPa geometry the harmonic isotope term $\ddF(\delta - \mathrm{diaspore})$ at 300~K is $+0.52$, $+0.77$ and $+0.83$~meV/atom (MatterSim, MACE, ORB), with $\Delta V = -0.30$ to $-0.32$~\AA$^3$/atom, which gives $+0.3$ to $+0.4$~GPa. Quasi-harmonic Gibbs energies at 10, 15 and 20~GPa, with one linear fit for both isotopes, place the 300~K transition of AlOOD 1.33~GPa (MACE) and 1.13~GPa (ORB) above that of AlOOH; a temperature-resolved rebuild gives $+1.33$, $+1.13$ and $+0.99$~GPa (MatterSim) at 300~K and $+0.94$, $+0.81$ and $+0.73$~GPa at 1000~K. These quasi-harmonic runs use a $\Gamma$-centred $q$ mesh with the point density of the screen, rounded to an integer (not necessarily even) between 4 and 12, rather than the shifted mesh of the screen. The $q$-mesh convergence of this shift was not tested. If its mesh error were of the size seen for goethite (0.1 to 0.2~meV/atom), it would move the shift by about 0.05 to 0.1~GPa through $\Delta P \approx -\delta G/\Delta V$ with the 0~GPa $\Delta V$; towards hydrogen-bond symmetrization, where modes soften, the error may be larger, so the sign and size of the shift are numerically unverified. The quasi-harmonic isotope term at 10, 15 and 20~GPa, 2.30, 2.42 and 2.22~meV/atom (MACE) and 1.86, 2.30 and 2.50 (ORB), is about three times its 0~GPa harmonic value, which is why the pressure shift exceeds the 0~GPa estimate. Its growth with pressure is consistent with the $\delta$-phase hydrogen bond strengthening towards symmetrization, exactly where harmonic physics is least reliable; O-H and O$\cdots$O along the pressure grid were not stored and are not reported.
 
 The models place the AlOOH transition at 8.76 (MACE, extrapolated below the 10~GPa grid point), 13.00 (ORB) and 15.14~GPa (MatterSim) at 300~K, a spread of 6.4~GPa; none of these values was compared with the experimental stability field~\cite{Ohtani2001} or with earlier DFT work~\cite{Tsuchiya2002}, and the experimental boundary is crossed at high temperature. $\delta$-AlOOH undergoes isotope-dependent proton disorder in the same pressure window~\cite{SanoFurukawa2008,SanoFurukawa2018}, which harmonic physics omits; the measured 4.4~GPa shift of that neighbouring transition shows that anharmonic isotope effects in this system are large, and the potentials overbind hydrogen bonds (Sec.~\ref{sec:tests}). The prediction is therefore an exploratory, conditional sign prediction outside the regime in which the rule was derived; neither its sign nor its size is validated. A logged search found no measurement or calculation of the isotope shift of this boundary. Related measured shifts point the same way, the $\delta$-AlOOH disorder transition~\cite{SanoFurukawa2018} and $\varepsilon$-FeOOD remaining asymmetric above the pressure at which $\varepsilon$-FeOOH symmetrises~\cite{Ikeda2025}, but both are different transitions. The same calculation for goethite to $\varepsilon$-FeOOH gives $+0.2$ to $+0.5$~GPa with a mesh sensitivity as large as the signal, so only its sign is stated.
 
