 \textit{Mineral D/H fractionation (ordering only).} The same isotope free energies give reduced partition function ratios, so differences between measured mineral-water fractionation factors test them without a model of water. Predictions for 118 structures were recorded before the measured values were read. The measured factors are attributed in our data file to Xu and Zheng for brucite~\cite{Xu1999}, Yapp for goethite~\cite{Yapp1987} and Hariya and Tsutsumi for manganite~\cite{Hariya1981}, each reached through secondary sources and not checked against the primary papers. The sign of every scored difference (6 of 6, in 2 scored pairs) and the order brucite $>$ goethite $>$ manganite in D affinity are right in all three models, and the mineral with the longer O-H bonds takes up less deuterium. The magnitudes are not: for brucite minus goethite the models give 190 to 300 per mil against a measured 22 to 46, 5 to 10 times too large, and the predicted difference falls with temperature while the measured one rises. The order is assembled from two temperature windows, and the factor of 5 to 10 applies to brucite minus goethite; the two pairs with manganite give ratios of 1.6 and 0.7, one of them unscored. Corrections to harmonic reduced partition function ratios are discussed in Ref.~\cite{LiuTossell2010}. Brucite (mean O-H 0.971~\AA) lies below the rule's regime. The scoring rule was recorded together with its result.
 
-\textit{Ice phase boundaries (failed).} The H$_2$O to D$_2$O shifts of three ice boundaries were predicted before any measured D$_2$O boundary was read. For Ih-II the models give $-12$ to $-140$~MPa at 230 to 250~K against a measured triple-point shift of $+12$ to $+15$~MPa; Ih-III is also wrong in sign in all three models, while II-III is right in sign and within a factor of 2 to 3. The measured values come from a secondary compilation and from Bridgman's D$_2$O work via a reference equation of state~\cite{Bridgman1935,Herrig2018}, with an inferred isotope label and no stated uncertainty. Both failures involve ice Ih, modelled through the ordered ice XI proxy, 7 to 9\% too dense in all three models; overbound hydrogen bonds and the omitted proton disorder are both consistent with the failure.
+\textit{Ice phase boundaries (failed).} The H$_2$O to D$_2$O shifts of three ice boundaries were predicted before any measured D$_2$O boundary was read. For Ih-II the models give $-12$ to $-140$~MPa at 230 to 250~K against a measured triple-point shift of $+12$ to $+15$~MPa; Ih-III is also wrong in sign in all three models, while II-III is right in sign and within a factor of 2 to 3. The comparison is not at matched conditions: a triple-point pressure shift also contains the isotope shift of the triple-point temperature times the slope of each boundary, so it equals a fixed-temperature boundary shift only when that temperature shift is negligible. Neither the model triple points nor matched-temperature measured boundaries were computed, so the wrong-sign verdict is as scored, not established at fixed temperature. The measured values come from a secondary compilation and from Bridgman's D$_2$O work via a reference equation of state~\cite{Bridgman1935,Herrig2018}, with an inferred isotope label and no stated uncertainty. Both failures involve ice Ih, modelled through the ordered ice XI proxy, 7 to 9\% too dense in all three models; overbound hydrogen bonds and the omitted proton disorder are both consistent with the failure.
 
 \textit{Prospective test (failed).} After the initial screen analysis we recorded predicted signs for 111 primary evaluations of pairs never computed before (42 pairs, 20 formulas, both phases experimental), after relaxation and before any phonon run. The bar was at least 75\% right, at least 15 points above the constant-sign baseline fixed in advance and a pair majority right in at least 80\% of pairs. The rule got 16 of 111 right, the baseline 5. 87 rows were lost to the predeclared numerical rules (69 failed or dynamically unstable, 18 not clean), dominated by KH$_2$PO$_4$-type and ice polymorphs with imaginary modes, which capped the attainable accuracy at 21.6\%. The surviving rows do not rescue the rule: on the 24 clean rows it was right in 16, the baseline fixed in advance in 5 and the opposite constant sign, chosen after the fact, in 19, and the pair majority was 5 right, 1 tie and 4 wrong. The failure combines numerical attrition with insufficient skill on the rows that survived. Transfer of the rule to new pairs is not shown, and the clean filter selects a population, ordered oxyhydroxides and hydroxides, close to the one on which the in-sample rule was built. Descriptively and after part of the data had been seen, the clean rows gave a total slope of 0.181 and a compensation of 0.44. The predeclared secondary test, at 600~K without a threshold, missed its 90\% bar (21 of 120); the 21 rows it scored right are all of its clean rows.
 
